import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/hgb/hgb308-309.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.summary}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  subtitle={metadata.nativeTitle}
  covers={metadata.covers}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope" },
        { id: "s3", label: "§308(1) – Uniform Measurement, Set by the Parent's Own Methods" },
        { id: "s4", label: "§308(2) – Restatement Required, With a Materiality Carve-Out" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Worked Example – Restating a Subsidiary's Measurement Method" },
        { id: "s6", label: "§308(3) – Disproportionate Cost Relief" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Worked Example – Relying on the Disproportionate Cost Relief" },
        { id: "s8", label: "§309(1) – Goodwill Amortisation: a Cross-Reference, Not a New Regime" },
        { id: "s9", label: "§309(2) – Releasing a Negative Difference to Income" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Worked Example – Releasing a Negative Difference in a Later Period" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Critical Judgments &amp; Practical Difficulties" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Relationship to IFRS (IFRS 10, IFRS 3)" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Related Provisions – Cross-Reference Map" },
        { id: "s15", label: "Quick-Reference Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  

  <div class="wrap">
<!-- ============ SECTION 1 ============ -->
  
  <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="callout info">RESOLVING AN EARLIER FLAG: the §§300–307 poster noted uncertainty about how a negative difference (bargain purchase) might later be released to income. §309(2) confirms this directly – a genuine release mechanism exists, covered fully in Section 10 below.</div>

  
      <div class="ctitle ci">A short but necessary Titel, tying up two loose ends from the previous poster</div>
      <div class="principle">§308 formalises the uniform-measurement precondition §300(2) already introduced in passing, giving it its own detailed rules and a genuine cost-benefit carve-out. §309 then supplies the SUBSEQUENT-PERIOD treatment of the goodwill and negative-difference items §301(3) created at initial capital consolidation – genuinely a SEQUEL to the previous poster's centerpiece mechanic, not a new topic.</div>
      <div class="elabel">HOW §§308–309 MAP TO GERMAN ACCOUNTING PRINCIPLES</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span><strong>Bewertungsstetigkeit, at group level</strong> – §308 requires the SAME measurement method to apply across every group member, not merely within each individual entity over time</span>
        <span><strong>Proportionality</strong> – §308(3)'s disproportionate-cost relief mirrors the same cost-benefit reasoning behind numerous earlier size-dependent and materiality-based reliefs throughout this series</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cs">
      <div class="ctitle cs">What §§308–309 require, at a glance</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-06-17"><span>§</span><span>Core requirement</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-06-17"><span>308</span><span>Uniform measurement per the parent's own methods; restate subsidiaries, unless immaterial or disproportionately costly</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-06-17"><span>309</span><span>Goodwill amortised per general rules; a negative difference may later be released to income under specific conditions</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 2 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Every group performing full consolidation under the previous Titel</div>
      <div class="principle">§§308–309 apply to every group preparing a Konzernabschluss under the Vollkonsolidierung mechanics established in the previous poster – §308 to the measurement of every consolidated item, §309 specifically to any goodwill or negative difference §301(3) created.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 3 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>§308(1) – Uniform Measurement, Set by the Parent's Own Methods</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">The parent's own choices become the group-wide standard</div>
      <div class="principle">§308(1) requires assets and liabilities included in the Konzernabschluss to be measured UNIFORMLY throughout the group, according to the methods the PARENT applies in ITS OWN individual annual accounts – formalising exactly the mechanic illustrated with the LIFO example in the earlier §§297–299 poster. The parent's own measurement elections effectively become the mandatory group-wide standard every subsidiary's figures must be conformed to.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 4 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>§308(2) – Restatement Required, With a Materiality Carve-Out</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Different methods must be conformed – unless the effect is genuinely immaterial</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§308(2) requires a subsidiary's own individual accounts, WHERE they use DIFFERENT measurement methods than the parent's, to be RESTATED to the parent's own methods before consolidation – UNLESS the resulting effect is of subordinate importance for giving a true and fair view of the group's position. This materiality carve-out echoes the SAME pattern seen in §304's own intragroup-profit-elimination relief in the previous poster: HGB consistently permits smaller, genuinely immaterial effects to be left unadjusted rather than demanding perfect uniformity regardless of cost.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 5 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Worked Example – Restating a Subsidiary's Measurement Method</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">A material difference, genuinely requiring restatement</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 1: A subsidiary using a different depreciation method</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A parent depreciates its own machinery using the straight-line method. A newly-acquired subsidiary has historically used a declining-balance method for its own, materially significant machinery holdings, producing a €340,000 difference in accumulated depreciation relative to what straight-line would show.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">Given the MATERIAL €340,000 difference, the subsidiary's machinery must be RESTATED to the straight-line method for consolidation purposes under §308(2) – the group's Konzernbilanz cannot present the SAME category of asset measured under two different depreciation conventions purely because of which legal entity happens to hold it.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 6 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>§308(3) – Disproportionate Cost Relief</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">A genuine, narrow cost-benefit escape valve</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§308(3) permits the restatement to uniform methods to be OMITTED where achieving it would involve DISPROPORTIONATE cost or delay – conceptually paralleling the §296(1) disproportionate-cost consolidation-SCOPE exclusion ground established in the earlier poster, but applied HERE to measurement uniformity specifically, rather than to whether an entity is included at all.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 7 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Worked Example – Relying on the Disproportionate Cost Relief</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">A genuinely high bar, not an ordinary convenience</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 2: A recently-acquired subsidiary with incompatible legacy inventory records</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A parent acquires a subsidiary mid-year whose legacy inventory management system cannot readily reconstruct the historical cost layers needed to restate its inventory measurement method to match the parent's own approach, within the consolidation reporting timeframe.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">PROVIDED the cost/delay of reconstructing this historical data is GENUINELY disproportionate – not merely inconvenient – the parent MAY rely on §308(3) to omit the restatement for the current period, while working toward genuine measurement uniformity for future periods as the subsidiary's systems are integrated.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 8 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>§309(1) – Goodwill Amortisation: a Cross-Reference, Not a New Regime</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">The exact same general rules already established for individual-entity goodwill</div>
      <div class="principle">§309(1) provides that the amortisation of goodwill recognised under §301(3) is determined according to the GENERAL provisions of the "Erster Abschnitt" (the general individual-accounts provisions, Book 3 Section 1) – meaning §246(1) sentence 4's mandatory amortisation rule, established at the very start of this poster series, applies IDENTICALLY to goodwill arising from capital consolidation. §309(1) is genuinely just a CROSS-REFERENCE confirming there is no separate, special amortisation regime for consolidation-goodwill specifically.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 9 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>§309(2) – Releasing a Negative Difference to Income</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">A genuine, if narrow, release mechanism for the equity item §301(3) creates</div>
      <div class="principle">§309(2) permits a negative difference (negativer Unterschiedsbetrag) shown on the equity/liabilities side under §301(3) to be released to income (ergebniswirksam aufgelöst) – but ONLY to the extent doing so is consistent with the principles of §297 and §298 in conjunction with the general provisions of the Erster Abschnitt. In practice, this is typically exercised where the expected unfavourable developments or expenses that originally justified treating the acquisition as a genuine "bargain" (rather than a measurement error) actually MATERIALISE in a later period – releasing the previously-parked equity item to income at that point.</div>
      <div class="callout suc">This confirms and completes the hedge flagged in the earlier §§300–307 poster: HGB's negative difference is NOT simply frozen in equity forever – it CAN eventually reach the Konzern-GuV, just on a DELAYED, conditions-based timeline, rather than the IMMEDIATE recognition IFRS 3 requires at the acquisition date itself.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 10 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Worked Example – Releasing a Negative Difference in a Later Period</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">The delayed, conditions-based release HGB requires, contrasted with IFRS 3's immediate recognition</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 3: The expected restructuring losses actually materialise</div>
        <div class="wk-body">Recall the distressed-sale acquisition from the earlier §§300–307 poster: a parent acquired a subsidiary for €2,000,000 against €2,600,000 of revalued net assets, recognising a €600,000 negative difference within group equity. The apparent bargain reflected the ACQUIRER's own anticipation of significant restructuring costs and expected operating losses at the subsidiary in the following two years. Those restructuring costs and losses now genuinely MATERIALISE as expected, totalling €600,000 over that period.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">As the anticipated unfavourable developments actually occur, the parent releases the €600,000 negative difference from group equity INTO the Konzern-GuV under §309(2), broadly OFFSETTING the restructuring costs/losses as they are recognised – achieving, over TWO OR MORE periods, something economically similar to what IFRS 3 would have recognised as an IMMEDIATE bargain-purchase gain in the acquisition year alone. The TIMING difference remains genuine and significant, even though the ultimate destination (the Konzern-GuV) is eventually the same.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 11 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Practical Difficulties</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
      <div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div>
          <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
          <div class="dot-list">
            <span>Whether a measurement difference between the parent and a subsidiary is genuinely material enough to require §308(2) restatement</span>
            <span>Whether releasing a negative difference under §309(2) genuinely remains consistent with §§297/298's true-and-fair objective, rather than simply smoothing reported results</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="elabel">PRACTICAL DIFFICULTIES</div>
          <div class="dot-list">
            <span>Reconstructing historical measurement data for a newly-acquired subsidiary within the consolidation reporting timeframe</span>
            <span>Tracking, over multiple periods, whether the specific unfavourable developments originally anticipated at acquisition have genuinely materialised, to justify a §309(2) release</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 12 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Relationship to IFRS (IFRS 10, IFRS 3)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison – HGB §§308–309 vs IFRS</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-12-1-1"><span>Feature</span><span>HGB</span><span>IFRS</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Uniform group-wide measurement</span><span>Required (§308(1)) – CONVERGED</span><span>Required (IFRS 10) – CONVERGED</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>Restatement cost-benefit relief</span><span>Explicit materiality and disproportionate-cost carve-outs (§308(2)-(3))</span><span>No explicit equivalent named in IFRS 10 itself, though general materiality applies</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Negative difference (bargain purchase)</span><span>DELAYED, conditions-based release to income (§309(2))</span><span>IMMEDIATE recognition in profit or loss at acquisition (IFRS 3)</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">§309(2) is the direct MECHANICAL consequence of the philosophical divergence already flagged in the previous poster – having chosen NOT to recognise a bargain purchase gain immediately, HGB necessarily needs some SUBSEQUENT mechanism to eventually resolve that parked equity item, which IFRS 3 simply never needs since it clears the item immediately at the point of acquisition.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 13 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">Where every §§308–309 event lands</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event / obligation</span><span>Document(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Subsidiary measurement restated (§308(2))</span><span><span class="badge bo">Konzernbilanz</span></span><span>Conforms subsidiary figures to the parent's own methods</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Goodwill amortisation (§309(1))</span><span><span class="badge bo">Konzernbilanz</span> <span class="badge bp">Konzern-GuV</span></span><span>Follows the same general rules as individual-entity goodwill</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Negative difference released (§309(2))</span><span><span class="badge bo">Konzernbilanz</span> <span class="badge bp">Konzern-GuV</span></span><span>Moves from group equity into group income, once conditions are met</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 14 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Related Provisions – Cross-Reference Map</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">How §§308–309 connect across HGB Book 3</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Provision</span><span>Nature of the connection</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>§300(2)</span><span>The general uniform-measurement precondition §308 now supplies detailed rules for</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>§301(3)</span><span>Creates the goodwill and negative difference §309 governs the SUBSEQUENT treatment of</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>§246(1) s.4</span><span>The general mandatory goodwill amortisation rule §309(1) cross-references directly</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>§296(1)</span><span>The consolidation-scope disproportionate-cost exclusion ground §308(3) conceptually parallels at the measurement level</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 15 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="three-col">
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Einheitliche Bewertung</div><div class="glossary-def">Uniform measurement – the requirement that every group member's figures conform to the parent's own chosen measurement methods.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Erster Abschnitt</div><div class="glossary-def">The general, individual-accounts provisions of Book 3, Section 1 – cross-referenced by §309(1) for goodwill amortisation mechanics.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Ergebniswirksam auflösen</div><div class="glossary-def">To release to income (through profit or loss) – the mechanism §309(2) permits for a negative difference, under specific conditions.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Erwartete ungünstige Entwicklungen</div><div class="glossary-def">Expected unfavourable developments – the typical justification for both an initial negative difference and its later release to income once they materialise.</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="legend">
    <div class="ltitle">Key</div>
    <div class="lrow">
      <span class="badge bo">Konzernbilanz</span> Consolidated balance sheet
      <span class="badge bp">Konzern-GuV</span> Consolidated income statement
    </div>
  </div>
  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
